#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111536} :activity/id #uuid "f15731b1-3f94-5cda-9865-241f1ae29ba5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050886 :topic/id #uuid "6e409d61-3310-5c51-abad-da7413b800ab" :topic/math-academy-id 1499} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111536 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111914 :tutorial/content "A biologist is experimenting with bacteria stored in a Petri dish. She observes that the number of bacteria in the dish *triples* every hour. If there were $100$ bacteria initially, can she determine how many bacteria will be present after $3$ hours?\n\nStarting with $100$ bacteria, she knows that this number *triples* after one hour. Remember that tripling means to multiply by a factor of $3$.\n\nTo get the number of bacteria after one hour, we multiply the initial amount by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\color{blue}{100\\cdot 3}\n$$\n\nAfter another hour ($2$ hours in total), the number above triples again. So to get the total number of bacteria after $2$ hours, we multiply the amount above by $3$:\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{100\\cdot 3}})\\cdot 3 = {\\color{red}{100 \\cdot 3^2}}\n$$\n\nAnd after another hour ($3$ hours total), the number above triples once again:\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}{100 \\cdot 3^2}})\\cdot 3 = 100 \\cdot 3^3\n$$\n\nIf we evaluate the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n100 \\cdot 3^3 = 100\\cdot 27 = 2\\,700\n$$\n\nSo after $3$ hours, there will be $2\\,700$ bacteria in the Petri dish.\n\nThis is an example of **exponential growth**. The number of bacteria triples over a time interval of $1$ hour, every hour. The total number of bacteria grows very rapidly, as we can see from the chart below.\n\n![](images/bf/bf039743a936cab0ca74708584fc3670202588d15fc63a4257ad1a737047da2d.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "60dc9833-8054-5a01-9e29-d592d2e4498a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8841 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "868a0a9f-3d0c-5548-b4c4-a2688c08805c" :step/math-academy-id 193608 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111537}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111537 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110625 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110331 :question/id #uuid "27d792f0-b574-531e-9907-80891a464ece" :question/math-academy-id "e-14250"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "affac6f0-da94-5545-a49c-e1e306bcd918" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049714}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111207}] :knowledge-point/title "Creating an Exponential Growth Expression"} :step/id #uuid "cc23439c-f454-5e68-a5a0-22cdf9bf2e0f" :step/math-academy-id 193609 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111538}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111538 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110626 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110332 :question/id #uuid "139799dc-8baf-5e41-9241-23e2ede77162" :question/math-academy-id "e-14254"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcd3e634-d64c-57dc-9d4c-8f7f67c731ab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050625}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487334}] :knowledge-point/title "Creating an Exponential Growth Expression Using Percentage Growth"} :step/id #uuid "8da3c630-d870-51f5-bf19-afa5e49a3cbb" :step/math-academy-id 193613}] :activity/title "Creating Exponential Growth Expressions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110858}]}
